this is a production of Cornell  University so today I'm going to be  talking about a project that I've been  working on for about the past year and a  half I'm just in the middle of it still  so don't have any end conclusions but so  first I'll start off by talking about  the project that I'm working under so  the pheno apps project is a group of  computer programmers and plant  scientists we're working together  directly with breeders to develop apps  smartphone apps for the phenotyping of  plants these apps will then be deployed  through breeder networks especially in  developing countries and one of the  crops that the phenotypes project is  especially interested in is cassava so  we talked a lot about cassava in this  department but I thought I'd go over it  again cassava is both an industrial and  a food crop and it's a staple for over  800 million people especially in the  tropics primary breeding targets of  cassava are pest and disease resistance  yield and field performance and dry  matter content today I'm going to be  focusing mostly on dry matter cotton so  because I have a root dry matter content  is the proportion or the percent of the  root that is not water so after the  water leaves the root it's so it's  typically around 20 to 45 percent and up  to 90 percent of that can be starch it  directly affects the dry yield of the  cassava roots and also the food quality  or the mouthfeel which then it which  then affects the consumer acceptance and  the variety adoption rate of cassava and  in talking to breeders I found over and  over that this trait is notoriously  difficult time consuming laborious to  measure the two methods that are  typically used are specific gravity and  the oven method the specific gravity  method here on Left involves weighing  the roots in air and water  and then plugging those values into an  equation to get the dry matter content  the oven method requires transport from  the field to a laboratory setting where  the samples are dried in an oven after  shredding and this requires consistent  electricity which might not always be  present in every given location and so  because of the challenges the time put  in and the labor put in different  breeding programs have been looking at a  new technique to phenotype dry matter  content a little quicker and so this is  near-infrared spectroscopy or nears so  it's been shown to be highly predictive  of dry matter in cassava roots and this  because of this it's being routinely  used at sea at in Colombia for the  typing of dry matter cassava  but the validated spectrometers that  have been looked at so far are  prohibitively expensive for most small  breeding programs to afford so because  of this we're looking into the use of a  portable handheld spectrometer called  the SIA which at $300 is a 150 to 200  times price reduction from these other  spectrometers cost so it does measure a  restricted amount of wavelengths in the  near-infrared spectrum area of the  electromagnetic spectrum but as I'll  talk about we've found this to also be  highly predictive of dry matter content  and then the spectrometer connects with  bluetooth to a smartphone app that can  then be used by breeders and technicians  in the field to collect data so now I'm  gonna take a little step back and talk  about what the infrared spectroscopy is  so this is a method that allows for a  quick and non-destructive analyses it's  been used in lots of different fields  including you can tell medications apart  like counterfeit or not counterfeit and  then in agriculture specifically it's  been used for cultivar identification  for measuring bricks and other traits  and what it is is the spectrometer  measures the reflectance of light in  near  for red region of the electromagnetic  spectrum which goes from about 700 and  the reflectance in this region  represents a complex signal from the  physical and chemical properties of  whatever is being scanned  so because the signal is complex  it requires multivariate statistical  techniques to be able to decipher what's  going on and then it also requires the  development of calibration models for  use in each and every application so  this cultivar identification in beans  will require a different model from  measuring bricks and apples and so in my  case this looks like three main steps  first the calibration data is collected  and then that data is fed as training  data into a prediction model development  pipeline and once those models are  developed they can be used for routine  analysis in breeding programs so this  requires protocol optimization to decide  what different steps need to be put into  the data collection protocol and also  into the model development pipeline and  then once the models are developed they  will also require consistent updating as  the phenotype change as environments  change so I'm gonna zoom in and talk  about my work in steps one two and the  protocol optimization so I have two  different breeding programs that I'm  working with ITA in Nigeria and not gree  in Uganda each with two different years  of data so far I just have this first  year collected my now sees for the rest  of the presentation will focus on these  trials in Nigeria which include 129  genotypes total with two reps each these  data were collected with six routes per  plot the roots were sliced in half  crossways and then scanned  cross-sectionally these are more slices  than were actually taken it was just cut  in half scan like this and then the  those same routes were put in through  the standard operating protocol for the  oven method to determine  content and this was done for each  individual route so for each route I  have a pairing of scans and a dry matter  content value from the oven method these  were then fed into pre-processing  techniques and then into my model  development pipeline which consisted of  50 iterations of random split of 70%  training and 30% validation to get a  better idea of what different training  sets well what the effect of different  training sets will have on the  predictive ability of the models and so  then I looked specifically at the  different pre-processing techniques and  their effect on the models themselves so  I looked at 12 different techniques  including scattered correction and  derivatives and compared them with the  raw data values so this graph shows from  left to right on the x-axis different  pre-processing techniques with raw data  on the far left the top is predictive  ability of the model with our squared  prediction in the bottom is root mean  squared error of prediction and as you  can see there's no statistically  significant difference between the  different pre-processing methods and the  raw data itself so I will use this to  inform my model development pipeline  moving forward but I'll keep checking it  with different data sets as the data  itself might require different  pre-processing methods so one other  thing that I looked at in the protocol  optimization is the number of routes  that I included for plot so if you  remember I had the dry matter content  for each individual route and I used  that in this analysis so from left to  right we have 1 through 6 routes per  plot and then this is the r-squared  prediction again and the root mean  squared error of prediction and there  are clear advantages to adding more  routes but there seem to be diminishing  returns after about three routes and  then this again will be used to inform  the future calibration data collection  and the SOPs that we develop for  breeding programs to  and their routine phenotyping of dry  matter contents and so with this our our  final goal is that routine use of this  method in breeding programs but to get  there we have Additional Protocol  optimization that I'd like to perform  and then additional data from the other  breeding program in Uganda as well as  from 2019 and then I'd like to evaluate  prediction models across environments  and breeding programs and then finally  integrate this data type and the models  that I create into cassava base so that  breeders can access I'd like to thank my  lab and my great collaborators from the  next-gen cassava project if you know  Epstein are there any questions for  Jenna Joan is how repeatable are these  did you try measuring the same roots two  or three or four times so I scanned each  root six times and so far have been  averaging the scans across the root but  I do want to look at the number of scans  per sample to see if that's affecting  the results that I'm getting so looking  at once can per root and comparing it  with another scan for the root or  averaging two or three to see how many  we should be so it's not something I've  looked at yet but I do  so the question is whether we're exactly  on the route the scans are taking place  and how that affects and the results  that so I've been we're taking the route  and cutting it in half crossways in the  middle and then taking three scans on  each half both on the cut surface so we  have not so far looked at different  regions of the route so we do know that  dry matter can vary from the proximal to  distal end of the route and that is  something else that we want to look at  so we do have a collaborator at Umbra in  Brazil who's looking at this right now  and I've been talking to him about  getting that data and looking at it but  so far the roots have all been the same  age roughly the same size and so that  also is not what is the relationship  between dry matter and consumer  acceptance so all that I know about that  is secondhand from readers and from  people who saw a few of them but they  all say that the higher dry matter is  better it just feels better in your  mouth it cooks better and so I think  that consumers can can tell whether a  route is higher low dry matter when  they're eating it and then it might mean  that they don't buy cassava from that  person who sold the low dry matter  cassava to them or something like that  but I haven't done any direct consumer  studies or anything like that any other  questions  all right on let's give Jenna another  round of applause  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at Cornell edu

